<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Azekah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tel Azekah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Azekah was an ancient fortified town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shephelah">Shephelah</a>, the low hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, at Tel Azekah in present-day Israel. It overlooked the Elah valley, the setting of the biblical story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goliath">Goliath</a>. One of the Lachish letters, written as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> advanced in 588–586 BCE, reports that the signals of Azekah could no longer be seen, suggesting that it had already fallen.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/azekah.jpg" alt="Azekah" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AVRAMGR · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TEL_AZEKA_A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
